Wait, actually, I think I might have made a mistake here. Let me double-check the coordinates.

In a regular hexagon centered at the origin, the coordinates can be determined using angles. Starting from point A at (s, 0, 0), each subsequent point is separated by 60 degrees.

So, point A: (s, 0, 0)
Point B: (s cos 60°, s sin 60°, 0) = (s/2, (s√3)/2, 0)
Point C: (s cos 120°, s sin 120°, 0) = (-s/2, (s√3)/2, 0)
Point D: (s cos 180°, s sin 180°, 0) = (-s, 0, 0)
Point E: (s cos 240°, s sin 240°, 0) = (-s/2, -(s√3)/2, 0)
Point F: (s cos 300°, s sin 300°, 0) = (s/2, -(s√3)/2, 0)

