Now, moving on to the second part: \(\left(\left(2^0\right)^1\right)^9\). Again, this is a nested exponent, so I'll work from the inside out.

Starting with the innermost exponent: \(2^0\). As I just calculated earlier, \(2^0 = 1\).

Substituting that back in, we have \(\left(1\right)^1\). What's 1 raised to the power of 1? That's just 1. So, \(\left(1\right)^1 = 1\).

Now, substituting that back into the expression, we have \(1^9\). Again, 1 raised to any power is still 1. So, \(1^9 = 1\).

So, the second part simplifies to 1 as well.

Now, putting it all together, the original expression is the sum of the first part and the second part:

\[1 + 1 = 2\]

